WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:09.950
Too gross. Too creepy. Too sad… or just
too bad. Sometimes a film dishes out more

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.549
than we can handle. Whether it’s crazy camera
work or unbearable scenes, there are plenty

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:21.170
of reasons that audiences prematurely head
to the exits. Here are a few films that were

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.890
just too hard to sit through.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.490
The Walk

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:30.490
Even seasoned film critics and industry insiders
felt the dizzying effects of French daredevil

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:36.720
Philippe Petit walking a tightrope between
Manhattan's Twin Towers in 2015’s The Walk.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.720
The 3D bio-pic recreates the notorious 1974
stunt in ways that past mediums, including

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.280
a memoir and a documentary, could not - and
that included making people leave the theater

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:54.520
to vomit. CBS News even advised people to
avoid large meals before attending a screening,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:58.370
reporting that at least 10% of audiences were
getting physically ill.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:03.100
All previous footage of Petit's heartstopping
stroll between the skyscrapers, which he completed

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.990
eight times over 45 minutes without a safety
net or harness, was captured from the ground

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.600
or the buildings. "The thing that neither
the book nor the documentary could do was

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.250
actually put the audience up on the wire with
Philippe," Director Robert Zemeckis told Vanity

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:24.450
Fair. "We did. And that is what movies are
all about." That and, apparently, barfing.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.590
Freaks

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:31.950
Director Tod Browning's pre-code horror film
about a 1932 circus proved so shocking that

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:37.009
the original copies were pulled from theaters
and destroyed, along with Browning's career.

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:41.700
In the movie, a gorgeous trapeze artist plans
to marry and then stop the leader of a group

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.950
of sideshow performers to gain his inheritance.
The plot pits the "normal" members of the

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:52.069
circus against the "freaks." Browning made
the bold choice of casting real circus folk

00:01:52.069 --> 00:01:55.819
with abnormalities to play the sideshow performers.

00:01:55.819 --> 00:01:59.280
Viewers were supposed to come away with the
message that one's looks do not equate to

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.719
one's morality, but things didn’t go as
planned. "It is impossible for the normal

00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:10.289
man or woman to sympathize with the aspiring
midget," said a contemporary review in Variety.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:15.220
The Hollywood Reporter called it "an outrageous
onslaught upon the feelings, the senses, the

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:21.060
brains and the stomachs of an audience." Mass
walkouts ensued. The film was banned in the

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.530
UK, and according to the book Horror Movie
Freak, one woman threatened to sue MGM, claiming

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.930
the movie made her miscarry.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:34.110
Comparatively, it's hard to comprehend that
level of loathing among today's audiences.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:40.010
American Horror Story: Freak Show took home
five Emmys in 2015. What a difference a day

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:40.920
makes.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.720
127 Hours

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:51.180
In 2003, outdoorsman Aron Ralston became trapped
in a slot canyon in Utah for 127 hours, his

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:57.000
right arm pinned by a boulder. To escape,
he sawed off his own arm with a dull pocketknife.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.100
In 2010, Director Danny Boyle released a movie
starring James Franco about the true story,

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.340
but when viewers arrived at that pivotal scene,
many couldn't hack it. Movieline compiled

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.930
reports of fainting, vomiting, and seizures
at film festivals and screenings across the

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.150
country.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.380
Franco told Vulture, "It's meant to be an
intense, personal, immersive experience. And

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.070
when he cuts off his arm, maybe it feels like
it's happening to you." Despite the suffering

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:27.730
of moviegoers, the film was nominated for
six Academy Awards, including best picture

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:32.470
and best actor, so let’s give the film a
hand. It really needs one.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:33.400
Irreversible

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:40.140
Don't be ashamed if this controversial 2002
French film makes you want to curl up in the

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.000
fetal position. Critic Roger Ebert gave the
movie three stars, but also called it "so

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.980
violent and cruel that most people will find
it unwatchable." He was right, but it wasn't

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:53.620
just the brutality that overwhelmed audiences;
there was also a scientific explanation for

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:58.760
their abhorrence. Director Gaspar Noé added
low-frequency background noise to the soundtrack

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.210
of his revenge thriller.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:05.770
Though such extreme bass waves are inaudible
to humans, the BBC reported that infrasound

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:11.460
can induce anxiety, extreme sorrow, heart
palpitations and shivering. "Naturally-occurring

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.980
infrasound has been associated with areas
of supernatural activity, as well as being

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:19.229
produced prior to natural disasters, such
as storms and earthquakes."

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:24.349
"Our response to certain kinds of noise is
something so profound in us that we can't

00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:29.759
switch it off," science writer Philip Ball
told the BBC. "Film composers know that and

00:04:29.759 --> 00:04:34.759
use it to shortcut the logical part of our
brain and get straight to the emotional centres."

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:39.219
The Paranormal Activity franchise and other
horror movies have allegedly used this tactic

00:04:39.219 --> 00:04:42.999
as well, so the next time you're watching
a movie between your fingers, don't fault

00:04:42.999 --> 00:04:45.990
your courage. Blame the fear frequency.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.349
Tree of Life

00:04:47.349 --> 00:04:51.919
While film scholars called Tree of Life a
masterpiece, the man on the street asked for

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:57.620
his money back. Terrence Malick's experimental
drama explores many themes: the creation of

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.800
the world, the meaning of life, the conflict
between science and religion, and the bond

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.710
between family... which is a lot for any movie
to handle. Tree of Life dropped the ball.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:11.599
Maybe it was just too much for the average
date night, because folks left the movie in

00:05:11.599 --> 00:05:15.770
droves. They weren't scared or grossed out.
They just didn't like it.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:21.039
Despite an 85% Metacritic rating, disputes
about refunds arose, and theaters scrambled

00:05:21.039 --> 00:05:25.930
to make their policies clear. According to
The New York Times, The Avon Theater in Connecticut

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:30.689
posted a sign urging customers to "read up
on the film before choosing to see it" because

00:05:30.689 --> 00:05:35.509
it was not issuing refunds. Other cinemas
agreed to reimbursements for those who left

00:05:35.509 --> 00:05:39.449
within 30 minutes, and Brad Pitt laughed all
the way to the bank.

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:40.319
Cloverfield

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:45.909
If this 2008 found-footage horror film makes
you lose your lunch, fear not, you're not

00:05:45.909 --> 00:05:52.289
alone. So many people got sick during screenings
that some cinemas began posting warning signs.

00:05:52.289 --> 00:05:56.849
In the movie, six Manhattan millennials are
attending a party when a gigantic monster

00:05:56.849 --> 00:06:02.439
attacks the city. Their experience is documented
on a personal video camera later recovered

00:06:02.439 --> 00:06:07.919
by the U.S. Department of Defense. The footage
is so jerky that bucketfuls of popcorn were

00:06:07.919 --> 00:06:12.909
regurgitated in cinema seats, bathrooms...
and the expanse of floor between the seats

00:06:12.909 --> 00:06:13.990
and the bathrooms.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:19.309
Because instances of nausea were so prevalent,
WebMD even wrote an article about the specific

00:06:19.309 --> 00:06:24.120
motion sickness phenomenon, stating, "While
watching Cloverfield, viewers were sitting

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:28.599
still in their seats, so their inner ear was
telling their body they were motionless. But

00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:34.219
the bumpy camera movements—and their eyes—misled
them into thinking they were moving erratically."

00:06:34.219 --> 00:06:39.479
Similar side effects occurred during screenings
of The Blair Witch Project in 1998.

00:06:39.479 --> 00:06:45.439
Fortunately, Cloverfield's first quasi-spinoff,
the 2016 hit 10 Cloverfield Lane, dispensed

00:06:45.439 --> 00:06:50.059
with the jiggly camera work, opting for good
old-fashioned jiggly John Goodman instead.

00:06:50.059 --> 00:06:54.680
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.610
you just saw. And leave us a comment!

